# Children of the Five Winds — The Sand Road

**Version:** 0.4.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings: Children of the Five Winds
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

Under such a deep blue sky, the Ide trader felt his voice could complement the sun and the mountains. "To sweep away a merchant's daughter," he sang, letting the echo be his companion until another voice called out, giving him pause. There was that story of the demon who used a traveler's echo to lure him into a canyon, the trader thought. Best ignore the voice. But wait…there is also the story of the oasis, the trader pondered, with a lonely djinni whispering for company, willing to grant riches for a good story or song… "Hello, cousin!" a very human voice said, cutting through the air. An Ujik hunter on a lean and athletic horse rounded the bend, pelts draped over the back of her horse, along with a freshly dead marmot, already drained of blood. Her unstrung bow hung by her side, and she carried a light spear, idly walking the butt along the ground. "I heard a song and thought of the djinni and the oasis, so I rode your way." "Hello!" The trader beamed, his heart settling now, no longer concerned about demons, djinn, deadly echoes, or riches. "I am Ide Katatsune, riding to Khanbulak. Where are you headed, cousin?" "Nomolun, a daughter of Erdene," the Ujik hunter replied. "I've business in Khanbulak too." Nomolun's horse easily trotted up to Katatsune's sturdy but slow mount, weighed down by packs, boxes, and bundles. "Shall we ride to Khanbulak together?" she said. They rode in silence for quite a while, stopping every so often to eat, to drink, or to rest. Katatsune noted his companion's furs and leathers, practical and well-worn, perhaps well loved. He himself wore a fur jacket over his Rokugani clothing, gifted to him by a merchant in Khanbulak. "You mentioned the story of the oasis and the djinni?" Katatsune asked, breaking the silence. "Have you heard the tale of the prince who fell in love with the water at an oasis?"

"Of course," Nomolun said, "but it's a good story, always fun to hear as one rides." "Oh, I had hoped you hadn't heard it. Sometimes a good story is better when the listener's unfamiliar with it." "Hmm, what about the one about the archer who threatened the moon for stealing her love?" "Oh, that is a good story too! Please, tell if you wish." "No, no, no. You're right. Stories are better when they are new," Nomolun said. "I'm sure I know one you don't. I'll remember one to entertain us." "Most likely I'll be the one to entertain," Katatsune said, with a gleam in his eye. "Among my peers, I'm the most knowledgeable in Rokugani stories." "Well, we all know all Rokugani stories came from the Ujik," Nomolun said with a grin. "Cousin, if you happen to find a story, Ujik or Rokugani, I haven't heard, then I'd happily buy you something at Khanbulak." "Really?" Nomolun replied, in such a way that Katatsune wondered if he had fallen into a trap. "There's a Rokugani robe I've always wanted…" "Don't underestimate me, cousin. We don't have to make this wager." "We have several days left before Khanbulak. Wagers and stories can make it shorter." "Well, there is a Qamarist scroll waiting for me there…" For the next few days and nights, the two offered and counteroffered different stories, revealing both to have collected a vast repertoire of tales. "How about the fairy woman in the kami-woven boat?" Katatsune offered. "Skin as pale as the moon and hair red as blood?" Nomolun said. "The entranced fisherfolk returned her to the sea, yes?"

"Well, perhaps the baby in the bamboo reed?" Nomolun nodded. "And the bamboo cutter found a piece of gold in bamboo every day after. How about the wolf and the crow who nursed the orphan to health? The queen who defeated the fifteen-headed demon?" In many ways, they did entertain themselves with this contest; they compared how they'd heard each story and how different their versions were. Yet both Katatsune and Nomolun felt the sting of pride. Surely one of them must know a story the other did not? "It doesn't seem we'll ever find a winner." Nomolun's grin had faded by this time. At their last camp, she had been quite surly. "I have taken all the air from the sky with my suggestions," Katatsune said, also exhausted, not by the trail but from their days-long back-and-forth. "Why don't you tell me the story of the archer, her love, and the moon?"

Nomolun spun back to their first conversation. "But you said you had already heard that one." "Ah, I did, didn't I…" The trader sifted through his memory. "But I'm out of stories to tell, and I have always found the Rokugani version lacking." "Oh, how does yours go?" "Much pining. Very careful. The love stares at the moon as the archer fires arrows near her with his poems tied to them." "Oh! In ours, the archer thinks the lover has run away with the moon, but there's always mystery in each telling." Nomolun's hands fluttered like birds, gesturing to unseen people. "Each storyteller changes the end as they see fit. Some say you can tell how much love has embittered their hearts by their ending…" Katatsune sighed in relief, mentally allocated some money for Nomolun's kimono, and let his imagination soar with Nomolun's words.

## Welcome to the Plains

At the dawn of the Emerald Empire, most of the Kami, the Divine Siblings who fell to the Mortal Realm from the stomach of their father, Lord Moon, set out to pull together the myriad groups of people scattered across the land to create what is Rokugan today. One of the siblings, Shinjo, chose another path: she took a group of her followers and left the lands they knew behind to see what lay far beyond the borders of the known. Centuries later, the descendants of these explorers returned, bringing with them new knowledge, cultural practices, traditions, appearances, and mannerisms—all collected from generations of seeking, searching, and learning. Now, members of the Unicorn Clan act as the ambassadors of Rokugan, maintaining connections and trade with those who live outside the Emperor's rule.

### WHAT IS IN THIS BOOK?

Children of the Five Winds is a guide to the Plains of Wind and Stone, the Burning Sands, and many of the cultures that live in those regions and even farther afield. This book covers a broad span of topics, including the Unicorn Clan; several groups outside of Rokugan; horses; meishōdō magic (also known as name magic); trade and language; and supernatural encounters, pacts, and talismans. This book's new techniques, artifacts, weapons, armor, titles, non-player characters, schools, and cultural information will help GMs and their players explore the vast plains and deserts that the Unicorn Clan has raced across for centuries.

The Way of Winds gives players and Game Masters information on the free-spirited and worldly Unicorn Clan and their five families: the Ide, Iuchi, Moto, Shinjo, and Utaku. This chapter also gives a fair amount of information on the Sand Road, the deserts and plains to the northwest of Unicorn lands, and the Ujik and Qamarist peoples that live therein.

The Blessings of the Sands offers players and game masters a new minor clan—the Fox—as well as new schools, techniques, items and equipment, patterns, and heritages. This part of the book focuses on new player options to bring to life games revolving around the Unicorn Clan, the Fox Clan, and the factions that they interact with that live outside of the borders of the Empire.

The Gifts of the World is primarily for game masters and offers information on trade, language, and otherworldly encounters. It includes new conditions and titles to use in games, as well as a large collection of new non-player characters and horse templates.

### The Way of Winds

Shinjo Kaede's parents watched their grown but not mature child scream at Matsukaze, the family's untamable horse. "Demon!" Kaede shouted. "You should have been named Demon!" Kaede, their right arm still in a sling, held a large stick in their left hand, ineffectually waving it about. The horse neighed and whinnied, mocking their feeble threat. Kaede's father frowned. "I am not sure which is more unruly." "Do you know the name other people call our child?" Kaede's mother said. "The Fire-Eyed Oni Kaede." "Hmph. They are rather willful…" "Others call them a terror." Kaede yelled, an animalistic bellow, unafraid of being kicked again. They threw the stick at the horse and it hopped away, neighing loudly. They stomped their feet until the dirt became a cloud around both of them. "This is all I can think of, to teach our child patience," Kaede's mother said. "Could we not…simply set them free, to be wild out in the wild?" their father said. "Dear, that would be cruel." "To Matsukaze or to Kaede?" he said with an exasperated chuckle.

## The Sand Road

In the middle of the ninth century, the Unicorn Clan were in dire straits. Although they had only returned to Rokugan just thirty years before, they had already begun to alienate their allies among the Great Clans, and their enemies were growing steadily bolder. For all their talk of coin's "tainting" effect on a samurai's heart, the Great Clans were wealthy beyond compare, and the Unicorn lagged far behind. They owed more favors than they could repay, and although they had crossed the desert and spent nearly eight hundred years beyond Rokugan's border, they had little to show that would give them clout in a land where strangers were distrusted. They were, as their few allies put it, "a camellia without roots." To survive, they would need to find a way to sustain the clan even in a hostile homeland. Newly ascending to become Great Khan, the Unicorn Clan Champion Shinjo Temujin promised his father, the hero Shinjo Nishijin, that he would find them a new source of wealth and power. In 845, he looked to the west and saw opportunity.

Temujin ordered his outriders to ride into the west and find a safe route to the World's Garden and the Conqueror's Sea. This route would wind through the Burning Sands and alongside the Ivory Kingdoms. Five years passed, and only half the scouts returned, but they carried with them a map of the safest route: a path that would soon become the Sand Road.

Temujin knew that if he could establish a safe, open, and well-defended route from the west, through the desert, to the traditionally closed and out-of-reach markets of the Empire, then the treasures of the west would flow like a river of gold to the lands of the Unicorn. Choosing the city of Khanbulak as his terminus, he secured the route with the loyal Moto family and ordered way stations built along the path, each one a half day's travel from the last. With shelter, protection against bandits, and maintenance paid for by tariffs, soon the Sand Road attracted merchants from all the surrounding lands. By taxing access, the Unicorn refilled their coffers, and soon they would fund many good works that would restore their lands to their former glory. Unicorn goods flowed to the western markets of the Caliphate and the Ivory Kingdoms, and wealth funneled back to Unicorn lands. What treasures they did not keep, they sold to the Imperial families and the other clans.

Today, the Sand Road is the largest trade route to Rokugan, carrying a near-constant stream of goods and riches. It has made Khanbulak the largest and most prosperous city in Unicorn lands, and it has cemented the Unicorn as one of the clans most valuable to the Empire's economy. Without the Sand Road, Rokugan's wealth would be only a fraction of what it is today, and very little would be known of the outside world. The Sand Road is a powerful tool for rulers who know how to use it.

### UNICORN TRADE ALONG THE SAND ROAD

As the primary benefactors of the trade route, and the only clan with access to the Sand Road, the Unicorn Clan are positioned to gain the most from the arrangement. Foreigners are forbidden by Imperial edict to wander on Rokugani soil, and that soil includes the many rich marketplaces across the Empire. If anyone outside Rokugan wants access to those markets and they cannot afford the costly and risky routes by sea, they must approach the Unicorn. Merchants pay tariffs to the clan to access the road, and the Unicorn have the first opportunity to divert goods to wherever they want.

Trade is not the only thing that comes down the Sand Road. Exposure to new ideas, culture, art, and inventions has benefited the Unicorn greatly, and the trust of the Imperial families allows the Unicorn to filter what they deem would be acceptable to the rest of the Empire. The vibrant cultural exchange along the Sand Road has led to wider stability throughout the Burning Sands, and so, to the Unicorn's credit, most neighboring lands see the road as a source of lasting peace. Indeed, new settlements frequently pop up near the Sand Road, relying on both the constant trade and the protection of the patrolling Moto family to survive.

The Moto in particular have seen a drastic increase in power and influence since the Sand Road's establishment. As the route's primary stewards, they are charged with defending and supplying the various way stations along it, and they receive a greater portion of its taxes. The Moto rely on the allied settlements along the route to aid them in keeping it free of bandits and hostile takeovers.

Only once has the Sand Road fallen out of Unicorn hands. In 1072, the caliph of Qamari, Ali al-Walid, seized a large portion of the Sand Road near the Hidden Valley of the Ganzu people, who were allies of the Moto family. It took two years for the White Guard and Battle Maidens to reclaim the territory and force a peace, and the Unicorn have closely guarded the road ever since.

The Unicorn's primary exports along the Sand Road are silks, teas, and work animals. Horses and riding ponies are among the trades in highest demand,

as the clan's reputation for excellent steeds reaches farther than the Empire. Some goods from the other Great Clans find their way along the Sand Road, but only after they have been obtained by the Unicorn. Rokugani art is quite popular throughout the Sand Road; it is considered both exquisite and exotic. There are markets in the Cradle of the World for Rokugani swordcraft and armor, which are valued as artful curios rather than purely as weapons. While many within the Great Clans are unaware of this, the quality of Rokugan's silk is the envy of neighboring nations; their silk is highly sought on the Sand Road. Porcelain is another highly valued item. Other than Yobanjin tribes and the people of the Land of Four Rivers (where porcelain was invented), the Rokugani are the only people who know how to make it. Finally, surplus rice is a major export to foreign markets, where it is often sold for three times its worth in Rokugani markets.

Without way stations, the Sand Road simply would not exist. These small, well-guarded outposts are equipped with storehouses, stables, water sources, and places to stay. The largest way stations are similar to small keeps, with aqueducts and protective walls, as well as a town thriving nearby. Traders may stay in a way station without paying, but the wise realize their tariffs are what contributes to each station's upkeep. Sadly, many way stations are not nearly as stocked or protected as they ought to be. Further, the Sand Road is nearly 12,500 li in length and runs across many different landforms, some of which are deadly. Even so, the journey is only possible due to the way stations, and perhaps one day, the last of the planned stations will finally be complete.

The vast Caliphate of the Qamarist faith is a nation that rivals Rokugan in size. It is spread across the mountains of Shem and Qamar and down into the "land between two rivers," the Cradle of the World. The great city of al-Zawira is the capital of the Nehiri people and a massive hub of wealth, knowledge, natural philosophy, and trade. Its people have an entangled past with the Unicorn Clan: the old Empire of Rempet, which stood here before the Caliphate, was broken by Shinjo and the Ki-Rin long ago. The city grows thanks in part to trade along the Sand Road, and the Unicorn are among the Caliphate's primary importers. Among the many goods the Qamarist Caliphate exports to the Empire is watered steel, often in the form of blades and other weaponry. Al-Zawira's tin-glazed pottery is in high demand throughout the Empire for its exquisite quality, as is its metallic-glazed lusterware. Al-Zawira's famous carpets and rugs are another high-demand export, contributing to the city's already thriving market, as carpet-weaving is an essential part of the region's culture. An unexpected high-demand item is hard soap, which is often exported in massive carts to Khanbulak, where it is hand carved into delicate shapes before it is sold throughout Rokugan's markets.

Additionally, exports like honey, lump sugar, spices, dried fruits such as dates and apricots, olive and walnut oils, thin papyrus (which, being made of animal hide, is rare in Rokugan), exotic seeds, bulb plants, and raw jade are all traded from the Caliphate to the Empire. The Unicorn also import artifacts like copper astrolabes and telescoping spyglasses, sometimes selling them on the secondary market or keeping them as curios. In exchange, in addition to the Empire's silks and teas, the Caliphate seeks various medicinal remedies for use in their potions. A lesser import, sake, is also growing popular in the city, although the Caliphate officially frowns on alcoholic consumption.

Despite being relatively close neighbors, Rokugan and the Ivory Kingdoms have very little contact. This is partially due to the tainted stretch of land forming a barrier between them: the Shadowlands, south of the Crab lands, border the haunted rainforests known to the Ivory Kingdoms as the Ghostlands. Some brave sailors of the Ivory Kingdoms attempt to reach the Emerald Empire along the Sea of Troubles, and a few Mantis Clan vessels have risked the trip to conduct trade. Still, most who seek trade with Rokugan rely instead on the Sand Road.

The largest import from the Ivory Kingdoms is jade, both as raw, uncut stone and in the form of artifacts such as dagger hilts or inlaid bronze pendants. Additional imports of spices, dyes, rubber plants, cotton, and precious stones such as lapis, coral, and pearl are also common. Spices and aromatic woods are especially important, as many are used for making incense, in addition to flavoring foods. Dark mahogany is especially valued and sells for a high price. While Rokugan has many glassworks, Ivory Kingdoms glassware is a highly valued luxury good, considered far superior for its beauty, clarity, and durability. Even small glass beads are prized, and priests note that prayer beads originating from the Ivory Kingdoms seem to be better at attracting the elemental spirits called kami. Transporting the most exquisite glassware is an expensive endeavor, but many powerful daimyō will gladly pay for a few samples, as they are impressive statements of status. Another highly valued import from the Ivory Kingdoms is medical texts. Medicine in Bhavyatapura, the City of Splendor, is far more advanced than the practices in Rokugan, and translated anatomy texts and restorative recipes are frequently sought by Rokugani libraries and temples. Examples of the kingdom's medicinal cures are also highly valued. Perhaps ironically, while the Ivory Kingdoms export gold to many other nations, there is little demand for the metal in Rokugan. The Empire has many of its own gold mines, and gold jewelry is not a common adornment in Rokugani culture. Like the Caliphate, the Ivory Kingdoms highly value the Empire's silks and teas, although their own domestic production of both is quite plentiful. From the Unicorn Clan, they seek riding ponies and work horses. While the Ivory Kingdoms are rich in gold, silver and copper are far rarer in their lands, and Rokugan meets some of this demand.

One unexpected major export is Rokugani hanabi, or fireworks, which are highly prized by the nobility in Bhavyatapura for their vivid color and unusual displays. The Unicorn regularly sell prepared festival fireworks to Ivory Kingdoms envoys, even trading with the Agasha family of the Dragon Clan to keep up with the high demand.

### UJIK TRADE ALONG THE SAND ROAD

The Ujik tribes have a strange relationship with the Sand Road. While they may conduct trade through the various way stations along it, most feel that their relationship with the Moto should supersede any taxes or fees for trading there. Some Ujik tribes prefer to raid the Sand Road rather than trade along it, while others, having learned that foreign traders have difficulty distinguishing them from the Moto, take advantage of this fact whenever they can.

Ujik who do trade often do so in raw wool or wool-based textiles. The Unicorn forbid any non-Unicorn from trading horses along the route (as the clan would prefer to be the only ones in that particular market), but the Ujik do sometimes disregard that decree, or attempt to bypass it by trading mules and hinnies, which are not technically horses.

Yet the most common thing the Ujik trade is their own protection. Several tribes are quite familiar with the lands surrounding the Sand Road, and Ujik-protected caravans are marginally more likely to arrive at their destination. While the Moto do not exactly encourage this trade, they have also been known to hire Ujik tribes to protect the way stations, especially when tensions with trade rivals are high.

### THE LAND OF FOUR RIVERS AND THE SAND ROAD

Many historians treat the Land of Four Rivers as the precursor to the Empire, for it was that land's culture that influenced the northern tribes who would one day follow the Kami. Some say that Shinsei himself was from that land and that the philosophy of the Five Rings may even have originated there. In the time that has passed since the Kami's arrival, Rokugan has blossomed into its own unique culture with its own traditions. Yet while the Land of Four Rivers remains isolated from much of the world for reasons one can only speculate on, now and again artifacts of that land find their way to the Sand Road. The Unicorn use their influence to obtain these artifacts whenever possible, not only for their great value, but as another connection to their people's ancient origins.

> **LOOKING THE OTHER WAY**
>
> Imperial edict explicitly forbids contact with foreigners, and trade with them is similarly forbidden. So why is the Sand Road even tolerated? It is no secret that the Unicorn are trading with nations beyond Rokugan's borders, so why are they allowed to do it?
> Perhaps the simplest reason is that the Empire's economy, and in fact its very way of life, depends on trade outside its borders. Importing and exporting goods allows the Empire's economy to grow larger than it would otherwise, while also offering access to items that are not native to Rokugan but upon which Rokugan's people nonetheless depend. It also allows them to trade surplus, which is important for maintaining the health of the Empire's markets. Diplomats of the Tortoise Clan have also advised that limited trade placates the more "aggressive" nations that would otherwise eye the Empire's wealth of resources: a steady flow of porcelain and silk goods may well prevent invasions that the Empire is unprepared to repel.
> It is also useful for the Imperial families to "overlook" foreign trade in specific places in order to contain it to those places. This allows them to control the degree of access without creating rampant unauthorized markets. The ever-present implication that Imperial intervention will stamp out markets that do not self-govern prevents these markets from growing out of control. This trade is often conducted on land that is technically outside the Emerald Empire, and therefore deemed not in violation of edict.
> The Unicorn are not the only clan to conduct such trade. The Phoenix and Dragon have limited trade with the Yobanjin peoples of the north, while the Mantis Clan retains contacts with faraway lands and many island nations off the coast.

The name "Ujik" is a title given to the myriad peoples and tribes living in the Burning Sands. These people have different spiritual beliefs, foods, and traditions, but all share a similar nomadic lifestyle and a deep appreciation for their horses and steeds. While skirmishes are common between tribes over hunting routes, water access, and territory, most Ujik are bound together by a series of interlocking treaties, marriages, and agreements. While there are too many tribes to name, some notable tribes are the Barga, Daur, and Nokhoi.

The Barga, also known as "the people at the land's end," are renowned explorers, traveling regularly beyond the borders of the Burning Sands. Their name is in reference to the myth that their ancestors traveled so far that they reached the end of the world, where they gazed down a cliff that dropped into the nothingness. There, it is said, something gazed back at them, and a Barga chief spoke with the being that held up the world. Some say this being took the form of a giant woman, carrying it like a basket over her head; others say it was a gazelle that held the round disk of the world delicately between two horns. While the Barga are secretive in nature, it is said that their extensive travels have gained them incredible knowledge and exotic items.

The Daur, also known as "those who seek," are excellent cartographers who dedicate themselves to mapping out every inch of the Burning Sands. They seek out the dead cities, crumbling temples, and hidden catacombs spread throughout the sands. Those who desire to travel the sands or find their hidden treasures and secrets go first to the Daur. Some even say that the Daur are in contact with some of the more unusual creatures of the desert, learning their languages and secrets.

The Nokhoi, also known as the "dog-eyed clan," are famous for their prowess in war and hunting alike. They are renowned for breeding exceptional hunting dogs. These large dogs have golden fur; pointed ears; and keen, wolflike eyes. Intelligent and loyal, the Ujik hunting dog is second only to the horse in Ujik society. As such, there are few Ujik who do not covet the beasts, which gives the Nokhoi tremendous sway among other tribes. Producing many great chiefs, warriors, and strategists, the Nokhoi are a force to be reckoned with in Ujik society.

Within each Ujik tribe are groups known as ordus, which are large extended families, or groups of families, connected by marriage or other agreements that make up a village. During the cooler months, these groups follow herds of musk deer, gazelle, and smaller game through the Burning Sands, stopping at the few oases dotted along the dunes.

However, during the hotter parts of the year, most Ujik tribes reconvene in underground settlements. Scattered throughout the Burning Sands, these settlements are carved into the living rock below the surface, or are premade structures that have been buried. For a non-Ujik to find such a settlement is nearly impossible, which gives the impression that these tribes simply disappear during these months. However, people with keen eyes and ears can sometimes spot the air ducts carved into the rock or spouting up from the dunes, the smoke of a cooking fire, or the echo of hearty laughter coming from below the sands.

It is during these months that many Ujik tribes hold court. Similarly to the Winter Court in Rokugan, the tribes discuss matters of great importance, including politics, marriages, and diplomacy. However, these courts are much less formal than the typical Rokugani affair, with boisterous storytelling, loud laughter, and the courting of youths who have been separated since the last year's reconvening.

The courts also serve to strengthen cultural ties between the ordus of each tribe. Since each ordu spends so much time traveling apart from its full tribe, the people of each tribe have a deep fear of losing who they are collectively. There is an Ujik parable that says that each ordu that sets out into the desert and does not return is like a thread pulled loose from a beautiful tapestry, and that with each thread lost, the pattern becomes more and more distorted, until one day nothing of it will be left.

The lands inhabited by the nomadic tribes of the Ujik stretch across the entirety of the Burning Sands. However, while the Ujik call these vast expanses "Ujik lands," their actual ownership is in constant question. Historically, cities have risen and fallen among the dunes, and regions owned by the Nehiri, Sogdan, Suhili, and Bandar have been shifted, abandoned, and lost over the centuries. While many fight over the ownership of lost cities and crumbling temples when something of value has been found, the Ujik do not recognize land as property that can be owned. Instead, they define their lands as "where one's kin set foot or hoof." While this attitude often puts them in conflict with their neighbors, the Ujik happily make every part of the desert their home.

> **THE LAND WHERE ONE'S KIN SET FOOT OR HOOF**
>
> In the eyes of the Ujik, Ujik lands are simply "where one's kin set foot or hoof." In this way, many Ujik consider the Unicorn lands in Rokugan to be Ujik lands, as the Moto family of the Unicorn derive from the Ujik. However, since the Ujik do not recognize land as property, this title holds more of a culturally significant meaning than anything else. In other words, while the Ujik believe Unicorn lands to be Ujik lands, this is in no way a claim to their territory; rather, it is a mark of the fondness the Ujik hold for the Moto, firmly claiming them as kin.

In the deepest regions of the desert, temperatures rise to obscene levels, baking whatever timid life remains skittering along the dunes, bleaching the bones of fallen gazelle and traveler alike. At times, massive pieces of jagged obsidian loom over the sand, sharp and gleaming like teeth, giving the landscape the feel of some long-slumbering terror. Peering into their ominous, mirrorlike sides, one can sometimes see carved hieroglyphics from the fallen cities of Rempet, worn smooth by the wind and sand. Some say they hold curses, others warnings, but either way, few other than the Ujik dare to pass under their shadows, leaving entire routes and wonders wholly unknown to those outside the clan. Fantastic ruins lie scattered across the desert, buried and uncovered again and again by the endless shifting of the sands. Sometimes the winds uncover pure marble domes, the caps of long-fallen towers that gleam like gems, welcoming travelers to rest within their bowl-like confines. An unwary traveler might also chance upon the bones of Rempet, abandoned cities that loom over the sand like a curse, the shadows of their sundered spires reaching out like fingers. The stones of the cities lie blackened by fire, a reminder of when they were burned to the ground by the rebellions that overthrew their pharaohs. Surrounding the dead cities of Rempet are massive statues half buried in the sand, their obsidian eyes gleaming, their outstretched hands large enough for a traveler to make camp in a cupped palm. Abandoned in the desert, one can find Sogdan ziggurats as well, leaning haphazardly or broken upon the dunes—or even Suhili temples, their cracked pillars standing in the sand like the tines of a comb. Leading down from these ruins there are often miles of catacombs, stretching under the dunes like worm tunnels, holding ancient secrets. Some of these hidden places contain the knowledge and riches of fallen peoples; others hold curses and creatures mercifully hidden from the light of day. There are also hidden caves under the dunes, stalactites and stalagmites stretching out from the maws of these hollows like teeth. Inside are remnants of yet more ancient peoples: handprints and drawings, or bones from a time even earlier than the pharaohs of Rempet. Sometimes accompanying these are signs of other, inhuman inhabitants: chambers carved into rock by clawed hands, insect carapaces the size of camels, human bones that bear the markings of teeth.

> **OASES**
>
> Dotted throughout the Burning Sands are oases nurtured by natural springs that give respite to humans and animals alike. Here on these lonely islands of life, one can find musk deer, argali, and gazelles shading themselves under hardy trees whose branches teem with beautifully feathered birds. It is rumored that fantastic creatures haunt these oases: manticores, ki-rin, massive snakes, and giant scorpions with human faces, all peacefully partaking of the water. It is said that should one see such a beast at an oasis, one should simply bow one's head in greeting and keep at a distance. Violence and bloodshed seem an affront to whatever force brought the oases into being, and should one disobey this unsaid rule, it will likely prompt terrible consequences. As such, even the most bad-tempered monster knows not to befoul such places with blood.

Many Ujik make semipermanent settlements in these places, building homes within the caves and ruins and revisiting them each year as they travel their routes. In this way, the Ujik breathe life into long-dead sites. All in all, the lands of the Ujik are vast, containing a multitude of hidden wonders and terrifying dangers. Building on the bones of ancient empires and even older secrets, the Ujik have adopted the desert and all it contains, delving into its mysteries and making it their own.

The Ujik, much like the Unicorn and the Moto, are renowned for their skill astride a horse, shooting arrows at prey from a galloping steed with terrifying accuracy. However, the Ujik are also known to use falcons, dogs, or other tamed canines to track their prey. It is not uncommon to see tamed jackals or desert wolves warming themselves by Ujik cooking fires at night or watching over their ordu. The sight of dogs and canines traveling with Ujik ordus has led to some stigma among the people of the Caliphate, who generally believe dogs to be unclean. Some of the particularly unkind have even taken to calling these Ujik tribesfolk "dog people," a name that has plagued them for centuries.

> **THE CAVE OF NIGHTS**
>
> One of the largest and most extensive of the Ujik settlements has been lovingly dubbed "the Cave of Nights." Its caves of volcanic glass stretch out for miles under the dunes; their high and glittering, arched ceilings reflect the light of cooking fires, candles, lamps, and torches, giving them the appearance of stars in the night sky. It is said that the Ten Lords of Death, looking for a way to protect the Ujik from the heat, made the Cave of Nights by capturing a piece of the night sky and placing it underground. In this way, the Ujik could enjoy the cool night even during the hottest days of summer. However, the twisting, rounded corridors have also prompted a conjecture that these tunnels were perhaps carved by a massive sand worm: one of the flesh-eating creatures said to dig deep beneath the dunes before rising up to swallow their victims alive. In this version of the myth, a great Ujik elder, desperate to save their people from the blistering heat, killed the worm in a great battle and claimed its burrow for their people. No matter how it was formed, the Cave of Nights, or Sand Worm's Burrow, has sheltered many hundreds of ordus for hundreds, if not thousands, of years. Here, deep within the dunes, those welcomed within may find hidden libraries, forgotten treasures, and ancient bas-reliefs carved into the glittering black glass.

However, while many among the Caliphate find the Ujik's association with dogs odd, it is said that some tribes have had more unconventional hunting partners. There are legends of a well-traveled Ujik warrior arriving home with "a creature who laughed." The creature was described as five times the size of a jackal, with striped flesh and a voice that spoke only in laughter. The stories hold that when the creature died, it was given a traditional Ujik horse burial somewhere within the Cave of Nights.

### THE BELIEFS OF THE UJIK

The Ujik tribes evince a wide array of beliefs. Some Ujik worship the forces of nature, revering the sun, which can bring both life and death in the desert, and giving thanks to the water that gathers in the oases. Some Ujik follow the word of the Nameless Prophet, though they maintain their independence from the Caliphate, and others believe in a spiritualism very similar to that found within Rokugan. Some carry a battered copy of the Tao of Shinsei, though their application and interpretations of its teachings differ greatly from what is generally accepted within the Emerald Empire. Some Ujik have even served as followers of marids, a type of powerful devil. However, the most common set of beliefs among the Ujik revolve around the Ten Lords of Death.

#### The Ten Lords of Death

Each of the Ten Lords of Death was once an Ujik elder, one who died honorably and was venerated, giving up the names they held in life. This practice serves as a reminder of the Ujik belief that all are equal in death; it is also a necessary step in escaping the cycle of rebirth. This is because the Ujik believe that since a person's name is the first thing they are given in life, it serves as their ultimate tether to this realm. Invoking one's name is to call to one's soul, and it is believed that at the end of a person's life, they will be called upon by name to either reincarnate or move on to the next realm. However, should a person give up their name voluntarily, it breaks this cycle, allowing their spirit to stay with their people as a caretaker and protector, riding eternally on a steed of white or gold as part of the honor guard of the Lords of Death. Or, should a person perfectly embody one of the ten honorable deaths at the time of their passing, their spirit may be given the chance to become a Lord of Death themself. In this case, the spirit is offered the mantle of lordship by the previous spirit. Should they accept, they become a new Lord of Death, allowing the previous spirit to pass on after their long years of service. Together, these Lords of Death guide the Ujik from beyond the grave to live great and honorable lives. While nameless by necessity, each of the Ten Lords of Death represents one of the ten honorable deaths venerated by the Ujik.

#### Death of the Warrior

One of the most honorable ways to die is to fall bravely in battle. This Lord of Death appears as an Ujik warrior wearing grand armor and impaled by a spear. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death grants the supplicant courage on the battlefield.

#### Death of the Guardian

To die so that others can live is a death that is particularly celebrated. This Lord of Death is often pictured as a burned figure holding the hands of small children while leading them to safety. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death grants the supplicant the wisdom and inner peace needed to sacrifice themself for others.

#### Death of the Rider

The Unicorn and the Ujik have a shared tradition known as kurichitai, whereby a person who knows they are nearing their life's end takes a horse and minimal supplies and rides out into the desert one last time. This Lord of Death is depicted as the leader of the honor guard that many faithful Ujik will one day join, riding on a horse of white and gold, its flaming hooves piercing the Heavens. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death grants the supplicant the strength to complete their last ride with honor and dignity.

#### Death of the Martyr

To die for one's culture, refusing to denounce one's blood and traditions, is an honorable death. This Lord of Death is often depicted smiling as a curved blade severs their head from their neck. It is said that if one prays to this Lord of Death, they may appear in a vision, carrying their own smiling head and offering wise words to the one who seeks them.

#### Death of the Selfless

To die in service to one's people, sacrificing oneself either spiritually or physically, is an honorable death. This Lord of Death is often represented with cloth covering their mouth and a determined look in their eyes. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death grants the supplicant the courage and inner peace necessary to do what needs to be done.

#### Death of the Child Bearer

The Ujik believe that death during childbirth is as honorable, if not more so, than death in battle. This Lord of Death is depicted smiling as they look down on the Mortal Realm and their living descendants. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death grants the supplicant healthy descendants.

#### Death of the Unyielding

To die in struggle with an unyielding heart in the face of overwhelming odds or unbeatable circumstances is an honorable death. This Lord of Death is depicted wearing chains that have been broken. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death grants the supplicant strength and endurance.

#### Death of the Strong-Spirited

To die while resisting the malign forces that lie hidden among the dunes is an honorable death. This Lord of Death is depicted in their last mortal moments slaying ghuls (a type of evil spirit), and it is often told that while corruption tried to turn their flesh after death, their great spirit resisted, and so their bones still lie peacefully somewhere out in the sands. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death during one's final moments protects one's body from rising again as a ghul.

#### Death of the Loyal

A death that is chosen instead of dishonor or betrayal is an honorable death. Among the Burning Sands are many dangers that would force a person to choose between their own life and betraying their ordu or clan. This Lord of Death is often depicted as a bloodied but smiling prisoner. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death grants the supplicant loyal children and underlings.

#### Death of the Long-Lived

A quiet death after a long and righteous life is an honorable death. While the Ujik are well acquainted with death and the honors it can bring, what greater honor can there be than to live a long life in service to one's tribe? This Lord of Death is depicted as an elder with tattoos denoting a storied history in battle and a way of dress that depicts them as a great chieftain of old. It is said that praying to this Lord of Death grants the supplicant health and longevity.

#### The Death of Ujik Horses

Like the Unicorn, the Ujik revere their horses, treating them with a respect and care that often exceeds that which they show for their fellow warriors. While most of a horse's body is cremated upon death, like the Ujik warrior it carried, it is common practice to save a piece of the body to bury separately in a place of honor. This piece is chosen to represent the horse's most notable characteristic. If it was considered particularly intelligent, its head is buried separately; if it was brave, then its heart is buried. Likewise, the long bones of the legs are saved for strength, or hooves for swiftness. Sometimes, with a horse of great legend, each of these pieces is saved and buried, and the spot marked with a banner or a simple pile of stones.

Spiritually, it is believed that by burying such pieces, the gift of these horses' extraordinary abilities is given back to the forces that bestowed them upon the living equines, hopefully to once again bestow them upon newer generations.

However, beyond its symbolic meaning, the practice probably arose due to the nomadic peoples' limited ability to secure burial grounds, as well as to the risk that buried corpses would not remain so. It is said that a creature who has died within the Burning Sands, especially around the deserted cities of Rempet, can sometimes rise again as a ghul. While most people think of a ghul as a creature formed from a human body, there have been cases of undead jackals attacking campsites at night, rotting camels rampaging through villages, and—yes—even horses rising again as terrible monsters.

### The Ujik and the Unicorn

Dressed in light leather and riding fine horses, members of the Unicorn Clan have distinctive clothing styles, equipment, and mannerisms identifiable to anyone who knows of them. This is because when Shinjo and the Unicorn Clan first made their way across the Burning Sands, it was the Ujik they first encountered. The Unicorn and the Ujik did battle, but, impressed by Shinjo and her leadership, the Ujik asked to accompany the Unicorn, pledging to her their loyalty and eventually becoming the forebears of the Moto family.

It is from the Ujik that the Unicorn learned their mastery of horses, their unique battle techniques, their galloping song, and their usage of the stirrup.

Those among the Ujik who did not follow their cousins back to Rokugan still think of the Unicorn and the Moto as family, welcoming them into their villages and offering hospitality without hesitation.

The Ujik are a hearty people, quick to laugh but fearsome in battle, with deeply ingrained notions of justice and honor that would remind any Rokugani warrior of a fellow samurai.

### Allies of the Ujik

Throughout the Qamarist Caliphate, there is little love for the Ujik. Many of the Caliphate's people see the Ujik as unclean due to their association with dogs, and they circulate rumors that the Ten Lords of Death require the Ujik to make human sacrifices or other such fearmongering. In return, the Ujik are standoffish toward the Qamari people, offering their services as guides through the sands as needed but parting as soon as their business is complete. Nevertheless, the Ujik have some allies whom they hold dear.

> **FANTASTIC LEGENDS OF THE BURNING SANDS**
>
> Tales of fantastical creatures abound throughout the Burning Sands. Many Rokugani know vague stories of the region: tales of djinn and beings part snake and part human, often told to children to warn them of the dangers beyond Rokugan's borders. However, here in the desert, even stranger tales are whispered among travelers. Stories tell of massive, flesh-eating worms that slither through the dunes, swallowing horse and rider alive before diving back down into the shifting sands. Or of giant scorpions with the torsos or faces of humans, mouths stretched into inhuman grins—or grimaces—by sharp, barbed mandibles, living in hives under the dunes. Among the greatest of these myths is that of the City of Knowledge.
> The dream of many scholars, the City of Knowledge lies abandoned somewhere out in the sands, deserted by an inhuman culture that had knowledge far beyond what humanity could then have achieved. Today, travelers delusional with heatstroke have been known to stagger into a village or settlement, claiming to have seen the City of Knowledge rise from the dunes. Many of these half dead and raving wanderers say the city stayed above the sands only long enough for them to spend the night, during which they wandered through endless libraries or peered at ancient, clicking machines before the fabled metropolis sank back under the sand.
> Some say that the reason the city has yet to be found is that it is constantly moving, only appearing by chance above the dunes. Perhaps it lies on the back of a giant sand worm or massive desert tortoise, or perhaps it moves by the power of those strange machines visitors claim to have seen within its walls.

#### Unicorn Clan

When Shinjo rode through the Burning Sands with the Unicorn Clan, it was the Ujik whom she first encountered. The two peoples did battle, but in doing so, they developed a deep respect and admiration for one another. The Ujik who vowed loyalty to Shinjo that day became the forebears of the Moto family, forever linking their tribe to the Unicorn. Thus, the Unicorn and the Ujik share blood and tradition; to this day, the Ujik consider the Unicorn cousins, welcoming them into their camps and ordus without hesitation. Indeed, they usually speak of the Unicorn as if they were simply another tribe of Ujik. Regretfully, due to the distance between the two peoples, there is little they can do for each other in terms of large-scale action. Still, both Ujik and Unicorn know that they have kin should they need them.

#### Ganzu

The Ganzu are the people of the crossroads. They are Unicorn but not Rokugani, and they share both Unicorn and Qamarist beliefs and traditions. Due to their shared traditions, the Ganzu are more tolerant of the Ujik than are other peoples of the Qamarist Caliphate. The Ujik can rely on the Ganzu to serve as a bridge between them and the Caliphate when tensions run high, and the Ganzu can rely on the Ujik's strength and expertise.

## Khanbulak

At the conclusion of Shinjo Khulan Khan's conquest of the Sand Road, she ordered her trusted friend Moto Qaro Khan to build a city on the border. The city would serve to watch and defend the Unicorn's unruly and untamable western border and act as the destination for all the overland trade roads coming in from the lands beyond Rokugan. Thus, in or around 1010 by Isawa reckoning, the existing trading post on the border was declared to be part of Khanbulak, the new City of the Khan, and a grand and impressive city was planned on the site. The most beautiful and desirable lands in the region were enclosed by great square walls. The first simple, earthen ramparts were then gradually replaced by shining white stone carted in from the mountains southwest of Khanbulak. A mighty castle was erected as well, where the Moto headquartered their White Guard. Meanwhile, Ujik and Qamari merchants were settling both the city and the designated outskirts. Temporary ordus and less-temporary caravanserais, even a Qamarist temple, sprang up on the other side of the border, swelling the city far beyond its walls. By 1072, Khanbulak was already both a military and economic powerhouse, facts which were put to the test during the Second Caliphate War. A new, adventurous caliph gathered a mighty army and attacked the Sand Road. Marching from way station to way station, the Caliphate army met no resistance capable of slowing its advance. Even the Ganzu's Hidden Valley was discovered and overwhelmed, the majority of the Ganzu warriors retreating on foot to Khanbulak. By the time the overextended Caliphate army reached Khanbulak, the city was fortified and more than capable of repulsing the attack. The Unicorn counterattack undid all of the Caliphate gains and threatened the very walls of al-Zawira.

### Trade With Foreigners

Along with founding the city, Shinjo Khulan Khan's directives formalized the border of Rokugan. Li after li of marking stones and cairns were erected across the sweeping western plains, where the Emperor's clear prohibition against unsanctioned foreigners could be enforced. But Khanbulak itself was built to straddle that line—half within, half without the borders of the Emerald Empire. Here, the Unicorn would engage in trade with even those foreigners forbidden from entering the Empire. Rokugani silk, tea, paper, art, and other goods flowed across the border, and foreign gems, gold, spices, coffee, opium, cotton, and all manner of strange goods and devices came in from the other direction. Even domestically, Khanbulak became the center of the hoofstock trade involving traveling herds. Horses, in particular, were brought to the city from across Unicorn rangelands, the Ujik plains, and even the Burning Sands. Many Moto and Ujik have grown wealthy from no other asset than their excellent eye for horses, arranging trades and breeding contracts to the great advantage of all parties. For all that it is one of the youngest cities in Rokugan, Khanbulak is perhaps the most important to the Unicorn Clan. Despite its status as the preeminent hub for foreign trade, Khanbulak is not entirely welcoming to foreigners. Due to Imperial decree, foreigners are not welcome in Rokugan, and the Moto governors of the city have never desired to draw Imperial sanction or displeasure—yet they also have no desire to discourage foreign trade, as it has made both their city and their clan rich. Therefore, the Moto maintain a compromise, permitting foreign presence within the walls during the day and forbidding them at night. As the sun sets, the town guards strike gongs hung over every gate, calling all foreigners to depart the city. Once darkness has fallen and the gates are closed, the Scarlet Banners patrol the streets. Any foreigners found in the city at night are presumed to be spies or saboteurs and generally put to death.

### City Government

Khanbulak's administration must balance a handful of sometimes-competing power blocs. The White Guard, headquartered in the castle, is responsible for the security of the entire border and always suspicious of the city's many foreign visitors. The merchant patrons, on the other hand, generally favor a more permissive approach to foreigners. Meanwhile, the clan magistrates are eager to keep the city scandal-free to avoid attracting the attention of their Emerald counterparts. And above all these concerns are the capricious whims of the city's nominal ruler, Moto Ogödei Khan. To govern Khanbulak is to carefully balance the desires of these sometimes-competing interests, not to mention the unruly presence of foreigners, dignitaries from other clans, and the often-fractious Moto and vassal ordus who camp outside the walls. The current governor is Moto Enkhar, a stout middle-aged man who is cousin to the khan. Enkhar's favorite phrase is "we shall see," which he uses to great effect to avoid promising anything to anyone. His secret weapon is his closest wife, Teru, whose smiling demeanor hides a razor-sharp mind and tongue worthy of the reputation of her birth family, the Yasuki of the Crab Clan.

> **CARAVANSERAI**
>
> A caravanserai is an inn that caters to caravans that ply the Sand Road. Most include a large stable, comfortable rooms, and plenty of food and drink to make themselves an attractive destination at the end of a long journey.

The military leaders of the city are Moto Rurame, the khan's sister and commander of the Scarlet Banners, and Temur Jurik, the logistical officer of the White Horde. Jurik, a member of a minor Ujik family, has risen to the rank of noyan based on his excellent record of discipline and his surprising facility with bookkeeping and paperwork. Jurik has very little true authority over the other noyans of the White Horde, who range across the expansive Moto and Unicorn territories, but in the absence of the official commander of the Castle of the Road's End (the Moto khan, who is seldom in residence), he is the commander of the castle and first among equals among the White Horde's officers. Jurik's great desire is to secure a worthy marriage now that he has reached the probable apex of his career, but his ambition in that regard may exceed the options afforded him by his extremely low birth.

The city's merchant patrons have no official organization and no official power in the city government. However, the eight most wealthy and influential patrons control an inordinate proportion of the city's wealth and trade between them, and they regularly meet in the home of Ide Yasu, the wealthiest and most influential of them all. Yasu's connections to her mother's Crane family have given her an excellent source of silk to trade with foreign merchants, and she is rumored to have taken unsavory steps to quash any serious competition, especially from the Badger Clan.

To the other clans, Khanbulak is the most remote and least appealing city in the Empire. After all, it is literally as far as it can get from the center of Rokugan. Indeed, only the Wall can be said to be less welcoming a posting, and at least everyone agrees that the Wall is important, as unpleasant as it may be. To most samurai of Rokugan, a posting to Khanbulak is the death of a career.

In fact, only a handful of clans bother to maintain a permanent presence in the city. The Crane, of course, would not dream of slighting their traditional allies by denying them a diplomatic presence in one of their most important cities—as perfunctory as that presence may be. Their delegate, Sasaki Kanehira, is making the best of his exile by investing in local businesses. The Scorpion delegation is understood to be present largely to keep an eye on the opium trade, although as always with the Empire's most secretive clan, there may be a hidden motive at work. Their ambassador, Bayushi Kensuke, wears a mask with a permanent smile and pitches their voice to match. The Badger and Tortoise both maintain a presence to keep tabs on the coming and going of foreigners in accordance with their clans' duties, and their ambassadors share a single modest house near the eastern gate. Even the Phoenix have lately dispatched a small diplomatic entourage, including at least one shugenja, for mysterious reasons of their own. The delegation is led by Asako Hajime, a scholar who has taken to the sudden availability of foreign books like a newborn foal to its legs: uncertainly at first, but with increasingly giddy enthusiasm.

In addition to hosting other clans, Khanbulak receives many visitors from outside of Rokugan. The largest foreign group is, naturally, Rokugan's closest neighbor: the Ujik of the Plains of Wind and Stone. These Ujik come and settle their ordus on the outskirts, just as the Moto Ujik do on the Rokugan side of the border. In the city, there is little way to tell the foreign Ujik from the rougher sort of Moto, and some have been known to flout the stricture against foreigners in the city overnight by claiming to be Moto vassals. The most prominent of the regular Ujik visitors are two brothers, Tahir and Uraz, who are fierce war leaders of their tribe. They offer their services as mercenaries to merchants and travelers on the Sand Road and dream of someday growing their tribe large and powerful enough to declare themselves khans—possibly by seizing Khanbulak from the Moto.

Many visitors also travel across the Sand Road from the Caliphate, mainly in caravans of camels and horses. Some have made permanent homes on the Khanbulak outskirts, building caravanserais to host their countryfolk or workshops to ply their trade. Foremost among these is mistress Hafsa bint Shadah, who owns a coffee house opposite the Qamarist temple. The grandmotherly woman's hospitality is legendary, but she has the political instincts of a pit viper, and important contacts in both al-Zawira and Khanbulak owe her favors.

Regular traders along the Sand Road know to keep Hafsa happy with frequent gifts and visits to her coffee shop, for she is a staunch ally and a terrible enemy in the ever-shifting battlefield of commerce. The Ki-Rin's road to the Ivory Kingdoms also starts in Khanbulak, and visitors from those nations are common as well. They even have one permanent, official ambassador in the person of Vazhuthi. Hailing from the City of Splendor, Bhavyatapura, Vazhuthi has a friendly demeanor that hides a certain brittleness—he has been happily serving in his post at Khanbulak for four years and now expects to be recalled home. But as the expected call to return fails to arrive, Vazhuthi worries that his political rivals in Bhavyatapura have conspired to effectively exile him to the cold, dry misery of the Ujik steppe. Other foreign visitors include occasional traders or exiles from the northern Yobanjin tribes, Tegensai pilgrims and fur traders from the Pillar of the Sky mountains, and rare explorers from even farther afield—who tend to assume they have reached just one more oasis city, not believing that in fact they stand on the edge of the fabled Emerald Empire.

Khanbulak's square walls enclose the most important and celebrated portions of the city, but even within their confines, the city has a certain sense of impermanence. Ujik-style tent dwellings are as common as Rokugani-style manors and row houses, and in the warm season, many "buildings" are little more than carpets or tapestries slung between poles to divide rooms. Still, inner Khanbulak has an orderly layout, with straight, clearly defined streets arranged in a sensible format as permitted by the modest lake and waterways within the walls. The northern portion of the city is anchored by the imposing bulk of the Castle of the Road's End, while the southern quarter sports the khan's palace, the sacred grove dedicated to the Lords of Death, and a small Shinseist temple. But right in the heart of the city, and arguably its true purpose for existing, is the Merchant's Street, where thousands of buyers and sellers throng every day from sunup to sundown.

The formal seat of the Moto family, the khan's palace would not be recognizable as such to anyone used to the splendors of Otosan Uchi or other major cities of Rokugan. In the Ujik style, the Khan's palace features few permanent buildings, being more of a carefully maintained garden overlooking the lake, with stone pillars from which to hang ornate tapestries and carpets to serve as walls. With roofs in short supply, the distinction between "indoors" and "outdoors" is thoroughly blurred, and the palace's floor plans can be adjusted by shifting the tent walls. Although the overall effect is both striking and comfortable, it tends to impress visitors from the other clans as uncultured. The khan himself seldom resides in the palace, preferring to travel with his ordu across the Moto steppe in pursuit of seasonal hunting opportunities. When he does return to the city, particularly during the winter months, the palace is lit up with hundreds of lanterns, even late into the night. The effect of the lantern light on the silk or wool "walls" is to make it seem the palace itself is made of gleaming gold. During these times, the khan's court is always busy, and the amount of trade overseen by the city is perhaps the real reason for the palace's nickname, "the Golden Palace." The khan's faithful steward, Bortë, maintains the palace in his absence. When dignitaries of sufficient stature to impress Bortë approach the city, he hastily assembles a suitable court from among the most esteemed people available, and Governor Enkhar receives them in the palace on the khan's behalf. Message riders are dispatched both before and after such events, so the khan might know whether he must trouble himself to visit the city over which he rules.

Traders and merchants fight for space along broad Merchant Street, which runs through the heart of the city; they constantly ply the city officials in charge of allocating places with favors or outright bribes. But the most desirable, most profitable sites in the city are not even on the street proper; rather, they are within the tremendous rotunda of the Sunlight Bazaar. The bazaar is a large, round building with a tall, domed roof supported by such delicate pillars that it seems from a distance to be floating in the air. As with many Ujik-style buildings, carpets and tapestries can be hung from the pillars to create walls, and so the space within the bazaar is routinely divided up into an array of attractive, often expensive shops and businesses. No city officials need be bribed to secure space in the Sunlight Bazaar, as the building is owned by the powerful merchant patron Ide Yasu, and space in her market is entirely at her whim. In the ordinary course of day-to-day business, her client merchant, Sosuke, administers the bazaar on her behalf. He also runs the rotunda's only permanent fixture: Yasu's silk shop, where both bolts of silk and finished garments are sold directly or wholesale agreements may be struck. Sosuke is assisted in his endeavors by a looming,

taciturn rōnin called Humble Joh, whose knowledge of the sword far exceeds his understanding of matters of commerce—and rivals his ability to roust unruly customers with a stern look and a tactically applied fist.

Nestled against the eastern wall, in a quiet section of the city, rests a small teahouse with a humble garden. Marked only by an artful arrangement of flowers in a vase outside the front door, it is known as the Evening Flower Teahouse. To a samurai accustomed to the comforts and elegance of teahouses in the great cities of the Crane and Scorpion Clans, the Evening Flower serves both as a welcome relief from the brutal strangeness of Khanbulak and as a sad reminder of all they must do without. The art adorning the walls is indifferent, the garden not quite as elegant as could be hoped, and the staff barely tolerable in carriage and deportment—but it must be said that the selection of teas is excellent, drawing from among the best available in the entire Empire. The Evening Flower is the project of Daidoji Kiyotaka. The middle-aged Crane—a robust man who embraces a Lion-style chonmage to disguise his receding hairline—has a perpetual air of defeat hanging around him, notable by his slumped shoulders and wavering gaze. When he was assigned as the Daidoji Trading Council liaison to Khanbulak twenty years ago, he thought it was a prestigious first posting for such a young man. Since then, he has realized he was sent to what the Crane consider an unimportant backwater to be forgotten, and so he has been. The Evening Flower is just his attempt to re-create some of the comforts of his home and keep himself from losing himself to unending boredom. Unless, of course, that is what Kiyotaka wants everyone to think. Governor Enkhar suspects that Kiyotaka is actually the head of a spy ring in Khanbulak but does not dare act against him without proof.

- Hook
- Rising Action
- Climax

### Lucky Donkey Sake House

The Lucky Donkey Sake House is a misnomer twice. First of all, the donkey for which it was named was slaughtered during an Ujik divination ritual, and its skull adorns the front entrance (although in fairness, the divination did predict good fortune for the construction of the sake house). Secondly, sake is the least of the alcoholic beverages on offer.

The Lucky Donkey is one of the city's foremost establishments for anyone who wishes to unwind, and it is not terribly concerned with propriety while they do it. The sake ranges from modestly priced to extravagant, but the airag (fermented mare's milk) is cheap, and a wide variety of other liquors can be had for the asking. Gambling is the other chief amusement of the house, and the clattering tiles or dice call many a visitor in off the street. Private rooms are available for private business, and a variety of services are available for hire—some legal, many not. While the Lucky Donkey does not sell recreational opium itself, there is usually at least one "customer" whose true business is the illicit sale of the drug, and the private rooms often serve as smoking parlors.

It is a barely concealed secret that the owner of the Lucky Donkey, called simply the White Lady, is the most powerful figure in Khanbulak's criminal underworld.

### The Grove of the Dead

Ujik priests of death occupy a strained position in their society. They are respected, yet feared, as death is both respected and feared. As such, some land in every Ujik settlement is set aside for the Lords of Death and their priests, but it is always removed in some way from the principal dwellings of the people. In a traditional ordu village, the door of every yurt faces south—but a death priest's tent is placed behind the others, with its door facing north. In Khanbulak, the Grove of the Dead is a patch of swampy land near the edge of the city, but still within its walls. The Grove's trees, carefully tended by the priests, are festooned with animal bones from ritualized sacrifices and even with some human bones of the most honored dead, interred according to Ujik tradition. Ten such trees are arrayed throughout the Grove, each a shrine to a different Lord of Death, and many Ujik come to pray or offer sacrifices at each shrine.

In the center of the Grove, on the highest, driest ground, lie the yurts of the priests. Any priests who happen to be in the city are invited to take up residence in the Grove, so the number of tents and people dwelling within changes day to day. The most reliable and revered priest in the Grove is the wizened Ujin Dayan herself, so gaunt that with her white face paint on, she resembles one of the skulls that she chatters

> **THE WHITE LADY**
>
> As the head of Khanbulak's criminal underworld, the White Lady is a dangerous person to deal with. She acts primarily through proxies and hired blades, and her true identity is a closely guarded secret. Some suspect she is a foreigner or even a ghost. Others fear she is one of the city's foremost, and wealthiest, merchant patrons.

with day in and day out. Dayan has forgotten more about medicine, herbs, and poisons than most people ever learn, and she has a fearsome reputation as a diviner as well—fearsome because unlike some, she never shies away from delivering bad news along with the good.

### Temple of Amaterasu's Rest

The Unicorn Clan is broadly tolerant of differing ways of thought and religious creeds, unusually so for Rokugan. This is nowhere more evident than in Khanbulak—but it is not the widespread presence of followers of the Lords of Death that showcases that tolerance. it is the relatively dramatic and proud Temple of Amaterasu's Rest, which hosts worship in both Fortunist and Shinseist traditions. The Moto do not, in general, pay much attention to either faith, so the presence of the temple cannot be seen as anything but a concession to the other families and clans of the Emerald Empire.

Visitors to this temple pass through a torii arch of lacquered black wood, then climb a small hill to reach the temple proper. A side path leads to the temple's administrative offices and residence for the staff, screened off by a stand of maple trees. Two stone guardians, a ki-rin and a fierce war dog, mark the end of the path and the entrance to the temple courtyard. The summit of the hill holds two modest shrines (one to Lady Sun, one to the kami of the land and water of Khanbulak) and an auxiliary building for ritual dances, sacred gatherings, and private ceremonies. The principal shrine is dedicated to Lady Sun, Amaterasu, but by choosing to celebrate her in the aspect of a sunset, the temple takes an almost scandalous approach—it declares the limit of her power, the end of her influence, in the forms of both night and the Emerald Empire's border, beyond which other gods hold sway. Perhaps there was no other choice for the westernmost temple to Lady Sun, but it is a decision that has won the temple few friends and virtually no pilgrims from elsewhere in Rokugan.

Although Khanbulak is a sizable city, the temple is seldom crowded. The head priest, Fanir, is an Ujik convert to Shinseism. Although he has a convert's zeal, he is well aware that his kin have little interest in Shinseism themselves. As a consequence, he makes little attempt to increase his temple's influence or congregation, but he is eager to discuss the finer theological points with any pilgrims or visitors who may suffer his attentions. He is especially interested in discussing how the Lords of Death integrate into Shinsei's teachings, perhaps hoping to find an agreeable solution that can unify the two faiths.

### Castle of the Road's End

Standing athwart the northern wall of Khanbulak is a modest but well-built castle that forms the key to the city's defenses. Given the castle's commanding view of the walls and the entire inner city, no army can truly claim to have captured Khanbulak unless the castle, too, has fallen, which has never happened in the admittedly brief (by Rokugani standards) history of the city.

The Castle of the Road's End stands atop a white stone platform the same height as the city's white walls. It consists of a single five-story keep and two adjoining towers, where the city walls reach the edge of the castle's platform. Entrance from the city is controlled by a fortified forecourt and gatehouse, which has a receiving hall and is generally as far as uninvited visitors are permitted to proceed.

Perhaps surprisingly for a Unicorn facility, the castle has no stables to speak of—instead, the ample stable and pasture facilities available elsewhere in the city are put to use. Mounts and carts can be fetched to the forecourt by grooms and servants whenever needed—they would be of limited use, except as rations, in a siege anyway. The castle does house the residences and offices of principal officers of the White Horde, most notably Temur Jurik Noyan; barracks for the castle garrison; an oversized armory (to hold supplies for the White Guard and Scarlet Banners); and the city prison.

The western tower houses the quarters for Jurik Noyan himself, as well as the grand map room, which always depicts the (seldom accurate) latest information on the locations of all the White Horde's forces. The eastern tower is reputed to house the Moto treasury somewhere in vaults beneath the castle itself.

## OUTSIDE THE WALLS

While Khanbulak is a large city by any measure, it could never be contained by its walls, were they ever so expansive. The free-ranging Ujik people would scarcely permit it, and their prodigious herds go wherever they go—they would never fit within the walls. Thus, the outskirts of Khanbulak constantly throng with an ever-shifting assortment of Ujik ordus, herds of hoofstock, and caravans from afar. This chaotic assortment can stretch for up to a day's ride in any direction, depending on the season and how densely the people choose to pack themselves.

The Khanbulak outskirts are only barely (or arguably) part of Khanbulak proper and may appear wholly anarchic to an outsider, but this is not in fact the case. The ordus are small villages unto themselves, each self-governing, and their interactions are strictly defined by the extensive traditions and customs of

their shared community. Those who flout the laws are delivered to their people to make recompense. Those with no people may simply vanish.

### The Qamarist Temple

Wherever Qamarists make their home, they gather for prayer and study of the Word. Wherever they gather for prayer and study of the Word, they eventually build a temple. Khanbulak is no different, and the spires of a Qamarist temple now rise outside the walls of the Unicorn city. Built of the same white stone that composes Khanbulak's walls, the temple is a modest affair—for now. A small fountain for ablution lies in the courtyard surrounded by scrubby little trees, and from this courtyard, several doors offer entry into the main building. The large common prayer room is divided by a simple screen of woven reed mats and topped by a broad dome. The interior of the dome is covered by an in-progress mosaic of brightly colored ceramic tiles. A scaffold rises to the dome, and a small team of artists and craftspeople can frequently be seen lying flat on their backs and affixing new tiles to the growing mosaic. The temple is the province of a pair of Qamarist teachers—they would reject the title "priest"—who serve as its stewards and as prayer leaders for the congregation. The pair are an Ujik married couple. The teachers are eager to spread the Word of the Prophet to others, with the eventual goal of building a new, even grander temple inside the walls of Khanbulak.

### The Horse Market

The most important form of wealth for Ujik, Moto or otherwise, is the herd. When a warrior joins the muster, they are expected to bring at least two, and usually four or five, horses with them, and it is rare for an ordu to have a herd smaller than their human population. So, of course, the largest group of human beings in the Moto steppe features the largest gathering of horses. Enormous paddocks with fieldstone walls dot the landscape outside the city, where livestock of every description can be gathered without fear of them wandering into another herd. The horse paddocks, of course, are the most important and prominent, and closest to the city proper. Here, breeders and trainers from the Golden Plains to the Cradle of the World come and conduct their business. The finer horse bloodlines are as carefully recorded and maintained as any human genealogy. Horse owners can sell not only their animals, but breeding rights or future foals, and particularly valuable stallions can be worth their weight in gold.

These deals are struck mainly between wizened, leather-faced oldsters leaning on the fences and staring fixedly at the animals all day. They are a mixture of samurai, foreigner, and peasant—in the market, one's knowledge of horses is the only measure of social status worth anything. Of all the leather-faced oldsters who lean on fences all day, the most leather-faced and respected of them all is Utaku Noriyori. Nominally retired, Noriyori was once the head breeder for the Utaku daimyō. Now, he spends his time at the horse market, sharing his wisdom with anyone who will listen and supply him with shōchū (he is not fond of airag, the most readily available local liquor).

### Al-Bekwari's Caravanserai

Where merchants go, there must be inns to accommodate them, and when the merchants come with caravans, the inns become expansive affairs with substantial courtyards for the muster, loading, and unloading of large numbers of travelers and beasts of burden. They have attached stables, many set aside for camels and horses both (kept separate, of course). They may even have adjoining warehouses, so the arriving merchants can keep an eye on their goods. Al-Bekwari's Caravanserai has all three: a courtyard, stables, and warehouses. A sprawling complex on a small rise, almost a li from Khanbulak's gates, the inn has two stories built in a tremendous open square around a central courtyard. The stone building is identical in architecture and plan to many of its cousins in the Caliphate, differing only in some of its Ujik decorations. The warehouse just behind its attached stables is fashioned of mud hardened on woven grass frames, an unsightly if efficient technique for a long, windowless structure useful only for keeping the wind, rain, or snow off of stored goods. The warehouse's position means that its main doors are sheltered from view, making it an attractive proposition for anyone wishing to move cargo discreetly. it is not clear whether al-Bekwari himself has any knowledge of the smugglers who avail themselves of his resources. The proprietor, Hassuf al-Bekwari, is a genial man to paying customers and guests, and a dour and suspicious one toward everyone else. A good Qamarist, he nonetheless finds the heathen faiths of the locals fascinating and has even contemplated taking an Ujik or Rokugani spouse—but he fears the censure of his community, especially of Hafsa bint Shadah, whose coffee shop is just downhill of his inn.

### Menghes Ordu

### Calamac Ordu

One of the largest Ujik ordus in residence belongs to the Menghes family, under the patriarchal leadership of Menghes Daidu. Daidu is a lean, long, sour-faced man, and his wives, brother, children, the children of his siblings, and grandchildren make up the majority of the ordu's population. The members of the Menghes family are excellent herders of sheep, and one of Daidu's wives, Bortelun, is a renowned weaver of blankets, carpets, and other woolen goods. The Menghes family is ambitious, hoping to secure high-ranking positions among the Moto family in Khanbulak or among the White Horde—or, at minimum, to secure good marriages and enough wealth to start their own ordus for Daidu's children.

Daidu's yurt is a large and impressive affair, its wool walls dyed a pale orange. A white horsehair banner flies from a pole near the tent's entrance, marking the family as followers of the Lords of Death. A small shrine dedicated to the Lord of Death that watches over warriors who have lost their lives in battle travels with the ordu; it includes an urn with a name: Orojin, Daidu's eldest son. Orojin was killed in a skirmish twelve years ago, and Menghes Daidu holds the Ujik Calamac family responsible. Daidu has petitioned the Moto khan for the right to pursue a blood feud, but Moto Ogödei is reluctant to allow it, either for his own reasons or because the Great Khan disapproves of Unicorn shedding Unicorn blood.

For now, the Menghes family endures the presence of its hated rival. But hot-blooded Menghes Benve and her brother Menghes Rom are both eager to turn the rivalry into fame and glory, and perhaps prestigious postings within the Moto military.

The Calamac ordu, an Ujik family, is every bit as large, prominent, and wealthy as its long-time rivals, the Menghes. The head of the Calamac family is Calamac Mala, a stout, ruddy-faced woman given to shouting and loud laughter. Her husbands and wives, children, and a smattering of grandchildren are the core of her ordu. Mala's wealth and her claim to fame derive from her impressive herds of cattle. The beef and leather from these animals have given her whole family a formidable stature and ready access to the equipment necessary to support warriors, and Mala has great ambitions for her family, expecting them to achieve high ranks in the Moto hierarchy based on their obvious strength and ferocity.

Mala's pale-blue yurt is only of modest size, but she spends little time in it, instead making herself a "guest" of various spouses and children as she rotates through her family. A large leather banner, painted with a crescent moon that serves as her personal mon, marks whichever yurt currently is playing host to the Calamac matriarch.

As suggested by her choice of mon, Mala and most of her family are Qamarist. The largest yurt in the ordu sits outside the regular line with its door facing west, toward the City of God. This yurt belongs to no one; it is used as a temple when the family gathers for prayer—and also as a feast hall on nights when the family slaughters one or more of their cattle.

A small sword stand sits within the temple-tent, holding the Ujik-style daishō of Menghes Orojin—swords that Mala refuses to return to the Menghes family, so bitter is the feud between them. If given half an opportunity, Mala will explain that Orojin died while in

her service and betrothed to her daughter, and therefore the swords are hers to do with as she pleases, and Menghes Daidu will not have them until he apologizes for withholding his warriors from the skirmish with barbarians, which Mala blames for Orojin's death.

Officially, the Calamac family rejects vengeance and a violent feud, in accord with the Prophet's will. Unofficially, younger members of the ordu are eager for any opportunity to further the conflict, especially sneering, superior Calamac Tygan and his cousin Yoo-lin.

Beginning one bowshot from the walls and proceeding for over a hundred li in either direction, the border stones mark the formal western edge of Rokugan. Each "stone" is either a single stone slab or a small cairn, but all are inscribed with the same words: "Here lies the land of the kami, where Hantei rules from the Emerald Throne. None who would disrupt Heaven's harmony are welcome here."

The border stones were erected contemporaneously with the foundation of Khanbulak, which was the first time the western border had ever been formally defined. Technically, this could have been read as a seizure of territory, since it barred Ujik nomads from crossing into lands they were accustomed to ranging from time to time. However, by clarifying the border, the Unicorn effectively permitted foreign presence anywhere on the other side of the line, rather than leaving the matter ambiguous and up to the whims of who had more swords in a given encounter. Although the majority of the border is unpeopled and defended only by patrols of the White Horde or Shinomen Rangers, most foreigners respect it. Those bandits, raiders, smugglers, and spies who do cross it do so at great peril, for the patrols move swiftly and are skilled trackers.

The Moto's White Horde is divided into several distinct units, called mingghans, each with its own commander, character, and remit. Nominally one thousand warriors strong each, these mingghans are almost always understrength, but there is one notable exception: the most famous and feared of these units, the White Guard.

The White Guard trace their origins to the disastrous march by Moto Tsume into the Shadowlands in the ninth century. The result of this heroic but doomed attack were the Dark Moto, cursed enemies of the Empire and eternal shame of the Moto family. Even worse—in the eyes of some devout Ujik—are the Dark Moto's defiance of the Lords of Death: they refuse to die. The remaining family members of

Tsume's party, along with younger siblings, heirs, and those left behind, formed the first White Guard in solemn remembrance of their kinfolk's folly. They were shunned and feared by all during their lifetimes. The White Guard have vowed never to repeat Tsume's recklessness and live instead as if they were already dead. While joining the White Guard is now considered a mark of high honor, for generations their notorious origins meant that members of the White Guard had no prospect of honor or advancement within their clan. It wasn't until the mingghan's critical role in the Battle of the Jealous Griffin that their reputation reversed. But while the White Guard are now honored, they are still feared. They dress in white and sometimes wear white face paint, like the priests of the Lords of Death. They purportedly fear nothing, even by the rather elevated standards of samurai, and each who falls in battle is celebrated and honored by the survivors. Their arrival on the battlefield is said to be the arrival of the Lords of Death themselves, for wherever they ride, death follows. While their fearsome reputation is possibly inflated, the White Guard are in fact among the best-equipped and best-trained warriors in the entire White Horde. Each warrior is mounted atop a powerful Moto charger, both horse and samurai armored in the best armor available to the clan. In addition to their daishō (katana and wakizashi), each member of the White Guard carries a long lance with a hook just under the tip for dismounting enemy riders. While they lack the endurance and mobility of Ujik or Shinjo horse archers, the White Guard can deliver a shocking cavalry charge that is equal to that of any other heavy cavalry in the world, save perhaps only the Utaku Battle Maidens. Today, the commander of the White Guard is the Moto khan's son and heir, Moto Chagatai. The eldest son of Moto Ogödei's second wife, Chagatai is a bold, ambitious officer, eager for glory and unafraid of claiming it loudly and publicly. He is happy to play the swaggering braggart, especially if it means others may underestimate him, and he is prone to treat every interaction as a competition—one that he expects to win. Under Chagatai's leadership, the White Guard have exercised responsibility for the security of Rokugan's western border and the Unicorn claim to the Sand Road. Bands of twenty to one hundred warriors ride long patrols to the farthest-flung Unicorn outposts or along the length of Rokugan's border from the Castle of the Badger as far south as the Shinomen Mori. It has been years since the White Guard were ever mustered in one place, but Chagatai longs for the day when he and his elite warriors are needed in battle once again.

> **THE BATTLE OF THE JEALOUS GRIFFIN**
>
> During the conquests of Shinjo Khulan Khan, the White Guard were conspicuously absent from the united Shinjo, Utaku, and Moto armies that brought Unicorn rule to the Sand Road. Instead, they were assigned to guard the home front, with the expectation that no misadventure—and hence no opportunity for glory—would befall them. Instead, the exiled warlord Batujin, once a Shinjo who dreamed of being khan, took advantage of his cousin's absence to attempt an invasion and coup. Batujin, who had taken the mysterious western animal called a griffin as his symbol, had found allies during his exile. He counted among their numbers a Yobanjin tribe, Tegensai and Ujik raiders, a powerful sorcerer, and—so the legends say—one hundred and eight trolls from the Pillar of the Sky mountains. Together, this army smashed every force the Unicorn Clan could muster, marching deep across the Golden Plains and assaulting Battle Maiden Castle. While the bulk of the Battle Maidens were away with their champion conquering the west, the castle's garrison was hard-pressed. As Batujin's trolls began to smash the castle gates with heavy iron clubs, all seemed utterly lost. At this point, the White Guard arrived, riding out from the setting sun in the west and howling their death chant. They charged through the Griffin's rear and smashed the few siege engines the enemy had bothered with. Not satisfied with this beginning, the White Guard withdrew, wheeled, and charged again, this time directly at the hardened infantry core of Batujin's army, where his trolls were cracking open the Unicorn gate. Half the White Guard died in the melee, but when the gate finally fell, it revealed every remaining Utaku Battle Maiden, mounted and armored. The Utaku warriors charged and reinforced the White Guard, shaking enemy morale, and one of the White Guard's officers, Moto Alakhai, knocked Batujin from his horse and trampled him to death. Batujin's army routed, his coalition collapsed, and the White Guard spent the next three months driving the stragglers back across the border.

### THE SCARLET BANNERS

Primarily comprising nomadic Ujik from the Moto family and their vassals, the Scarlet Banners are ill at ease in the city that is their responsibility. This mingghan of the White Horde is second only to the White Guard in its access to warriors and matériel, but many of her rank and file would gladly exchange their posting for one less prestigious. In the opinion of Moto Rurame Noyan, the Scarlet Banners' commander, the Moto do not belong in cities—and it is an opinion many of her warriors share.

Perhaps that is why the Scarlet Banners so aggressively extend their remit to include the rangelands surrounding their city. As the city exceeds its walls, so too do the Banners patrol far past sight of their official headquarters. Per Moto Rurame's command, the Banners consider everywhere within two days' ride of Khanbulak to be under their protection, and any ordu or traveler who happens across a Scarlet Banners patrol is expected to comply with its authority.

In truth, without the orderly villages and provinces of inner Rokugan, proper magistrates are few and far between on the Moto steppe. The Scarlet Banners, therefore, perform many of the same duties—duties for which Moto Rurame ensures all her officers are trained.

Scarlet Banners officers can interpret and quote the relevant laws, track and capture criminals, and dispense the rough, frontier-style justice preferred by the Moto.

In the course of their duties, the Scarlet Banners favor light armor; fast, hardy horses; and a mixture of weapons as well suited to a hunter as to a warrior. Each Banner is expected to be able to fire a bow or throw a javelin from horseback, and each has some form of sidearm, whether a cudgel, scimitar, or magistrate's jitte. But the truly versatile and dangerous tool in the Scarlet Banners' arsenal is the lariat, a simple length of rope with a loop at the end. Used to capture, break, and tame wild hoofstock, particularly horses, a lariat is also an excellent tool for capturing fleeing criminals or spies. An expert user can tangle a foe's foot or even throat in the loop, pulling the rope tight with a quick jerk. The lariat can also be tied to a horse's saddle, meaning that anyone who might try to pull away must pull against not the strength of a human's arm, but of a few hundred pounds of irate horse.

Although peacekeeping and law enforcement keep the mingghan more than busy, the Scarlet Banners are still a military unit, and they long for a chance to fight a proper battle against a proper enemy. But unless Khanbulak is attacked by some foreign power, that seems unlikely to happen anytime soon.

## The Ganzu of the Hidden Valley

As denizens of the most valuable oasis in the Burning Sands, the Ganzu people are among the Unicorn's most important allies along the Sand Road. As vassals of the Shinjo family and allies of the Moto, the Ganzu protect the Hidden Valley from those who would seize its bounty for themselves. They are a people of the crossroads, exploring the world as it comes to them from either end of the Sand Road.

### Customs

The Ganzu people have little reason to venture beyond the fertile river valley of their home. Vast deserts of sand and sunbaked clay await them in all directions. For protection from the unrelenting heat, Ganzu homes are mostly underground, and even the tallest spires of their city reach to just beneath the tops of the canyon walls. From the horizon, the city is invisible; none would suspect the thriving civilization just beneath the canyon lip until they were upon it.

The Ganzu people have much of their cultural history in common with the Ujik tribes of the steppes and Burning Sands. However, as time has passed, they have assimilated the cultural artifacts that have passed through the Hidden Valley. Their homes display a mix of eastern and western sensibilities, and their cultural norms are syncretic with both Ujik and al-Zawiran customs. Many Ganzu have embraced the Qamarist religion; temples to the one God of the Nameless Prophet dot the hidden city. But they still tell the folk stories of their old religions, and their old gods are seen through the lens of new beliefs. They see no contradiction in the marriage of the Word of the Prophet and a continued belief in the Lords of Death or in the wisdom of the Little Teacher.

### Arts

As peoples of an important desert crossroads, the Ganzu have adapted many arts into their repertoire. Among the most important is dance: folk dances are a cultural touchstone and pastime, and many religious dances are practiced throughout the year. Another revered art is that of the storyteller: the bard enjoys a high position of cultural importance. The bard must have a vast repository of stories memorized and ready to present at a moment's notice. During seated performances, the Ganzu bard employs props and shadow puppetry to further enhance their tales, which are commonly based on historical events, local folklore, or legend.

### Defense

The Hidden Valley is the only oasis between al-Zawira and Khanbulak along the Sand Road, and thus it is a tempting target for any who wish to seize control of the route. This happened once during the Second Caliphate War, and the Ganzu have taken their defeat to heart. Ganzu warriors are primarily known for martial skill with axes, the ax being regarded as an important and versatile tool in their culture, the warrior's tie to the farmer and logger.
